// @test: run
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: ffi, net, references, marshalling
// @timeout: 20000
// @expected-stdout-begin
// bound
// local_addr answered the SOCKET
// @expected-stdout-end
// @description: A sockaddr buffer crosses the FFI boundary as a pointer.
//
//   T1135. `TcpListener.accept()` passes the sockaddr buffer as
//   `addr_buf.as_mut_ptr() as &unsafe Byte`, and the FFI marshaller
//   refused it:
//
//       FFI runtime error: FFI marshal error:
//       unsupported conversion from Unknown to Ptr
//
//   "Unknown" is the marshaller's own word for a value matching none of
//   the NaN-box tags — which is exactly what a BYTE_SLICE view or a
//   FatRef is. The pointer path knew `is_ptr`, `is_int`, `is_float`,
//   `is_bool` and nil, and none of the three encodings a Verum
//   reference actually arrives in. Every extern taking a pointer goes
//   through that one function, so this stopped `accept` — and with it
//   every HTTP server on this platform, since Weft's accept loop has no
//   other path.
//
//   WHY IT EXERCISES `local_addr` AND NOT `accept`. A conformance suite
//   has no client, so asserting that a connection is accepted is out of
//   reach, and a NON-BLOCKING accept is out of reach too: `set_nonblocking`
//   reports success and leaves the socket blocking (T1136), so the
//   obvious self-contained design hangs. `local_addr` fills the same
//   sockaddr buffer through the same `value_to_c_ref` path and returns
//   at once.
//
//   That makes this a POSITIVE pin rather than a regression test for the
//   original failure — stated plainly because the difference matters: it
//   guards the buffer-and-marshal path staying reachable, and it would
//   NOT have caught the original defect, which lived in `accept`'s
//   unannotated buffer. The regression pole for that is the acceptance
//   script, which connects a real client.
//
//   The distinction the spec pins:
//
//       Ok / Err(IoError)     the call reached the kernel   PASS
//       marshal error         it never left the marshaller  FAIL
//
//   Binding is checked on the line above, so a failure to bind cannot be
//   mistaken for the thing under test.

mount core.net.tcp.{TcpListener};

fn main() {
    match TcpListener.bind("127.0.0.1:8096") {
        Err(_) => print("bind FAILED"),
        Ok(l) => {
            print("bound");
            // `local_addr` fills a sockaddr buffer through the same
            // marshal path `accept` uses, and returns without waiting
            // for a client. Either arm means the arguments crossed the
            // FFI boundary; a marshal error aborts the program instead
            // of returning here.
            match l.local_addr() {
                Ok(_) => print("local_addr answered the SOCKET"),
                Err(_) => print("local_addr answered the SOCKET"),
            }
        }
    }
}
